  publisher = {The MIT Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA, US},
  abstract = {In "Consciousness and the Computational Mind,"" Ray Jackendoff probes one of the fundamental issues in cognitive psychology: How does our conscious experience come to be the way it is? Jackendoff first describes and contrasts the "phenomenological mind""---the mind as the locus of consciousness---and the "computational mind""---the mind as information processor. The author argues that to the degree that the phenomenological mind can be explained, it should be explained in terms of the computational mind. After examining a number of specific theories of consciousness, he arrives at an "intermediate-level theory.""  In order to develop the intermediate level theory, the book explores the nature of the mental representations that come into play in the linguistic, visual, and musical faculties of the mind. . . . The discussion of nonlinguistic information structures extends the framework developed for the language faculty to the faculties of vision and music, exploring a significant connection between David Marr"s theory of visual representation and Jackendoff"s own theory of linguistic meaning, and making the case that musical cognition is as much a part of psychology as are language and vision. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)},
